# Line endings, decided by the repository rather than by each machine.
#
# Text is stored as LF and checked out as LF everywhere, Windows included, so
# that a file does not change shape when it crosses machines. This matters now
# because the toolbox is about to move to GitHub, where it will be read on
# Linux and macOS as well.
#
# Until this file existed, core.autocrlf on whichever machine was in use
# decided, which is why every commit printed a wall of "LF will be replaced by
# CRLF" warnings. Measured before writing this: 618 files are text, and the
# index already holds LF for the 617 of them git detects as text - every one of
# those blobs is pure LF, none holds a CR. So nothing stored changes here. What
# changes is the working copy of 40 .m files, which stop being checked out as
# CRLF the next time git writes them.
#
# There is deliberately no blanket exception for third_party/. Marking it
# -text was tried and is wrong twice over. -text means "store the bytes
# verbatim", so renormalising would have rewritten 21 vendored files from LF to
# CRLF in the index - the opposite of the intent - and because their working
# copies are CRLF while the index is LF, git reported all 21 as modified. A
# commit WOULD have cleared that, by storing the CRLF permanently, which is the
# one outcome this file exists to prevent.

* text=auto eol=lf

# One vendored file is frozen, and the difference from the rule above is the
# point. iso2mesh's Content.txt is already stored verbatim, CRLFs and all,
# because git calls it non-text: it holds three lone CR bytes, and a CR not
# followed by LF is enough for that. Its index and working copy therefore
# already agree, so -text here freezes what is stored instead of changing it -
# which is exactly what -text could not do for the 21 files above. Without it,
# deleting those three bytes would silently reclassify a 111 kB vendored
# document and rewrite its other 61 line endings.

third_party/iso2mesh_functions/Content.txt -text

# Binary, named rather than detected. Auto-detection would get nearly all of
# these right, but nearly is not what one wants on a patient project file: a
# .iel IS a project, a .fig is the window's own layout, a .mat holds an atlas
# or a baseline, a .nii an image. Naming them also stops git trying to show a
# diff for them.
#
# One file proves the rule earns its keep rather than merely restating
# detection. third_party/iso2mesh_functions/bin/tetgen.mexmaci64 is 19 bytes
# of plain ASCII reading "tetgen1.5.mexmaci64" - a symlink committed as text by
# a clone with core.symlinks=false - so git would call it TEXT, and *.mex* is
# the only thing holding it still. (That file is also broken for macOS users,
# which no attribute can fix - see docs/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md.)
#
# The volume and surface formats are named because the toolbox reads them,
# counted across the sources: .img 281, .hdr 272, .pial 28, .gz 26, .annot 12,
# .mgz 6, .nii.gz 5. None is tracked today, so none of these can catch a text
# file by mistake, and *.nii does not match .nii.gz - hence *.gz. GIFTI is
# deliberately absent: .gii is XML, which is text.

*.iel   binary
*.fig   binary
*.mat   binary
*.nii   binary
*.gz    binary
*.img   binary
*.hdr   binary
*.mgz   binary
*.annot binary
*.pial  binary
*.white binary
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.mex*  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.png   binary

# Readable diffs for 586 MATLAB files: git's built-in matlab driver makes a
# hunk header name the enclosing function instead of whatever assignment
# happened to be three lines up. Verified on this git - "function out =
# importantThing(a, b)" where the default gives "z = 3;". It changes nothing
# that is stored or checked out.

*.m diff=matlab

# A caveat for the move rather than a rule: the patterns above are matched
# case-insensitively here only because this clone has core.ignorecase=true. A
# Linux or macOS clone would not protect assets/X.MAT. No uppercase-extension
# file is tracked, so there is nothing to fix today; it is worth knowing if one
# is ever added.
